You can give yourself a spine without turning pain into a theory that some people are less human.

There are a few separate claims tangled together here, and they need to be split cleanly.

First: “I am my pain.” That can be a powerful identity statement if you mean, “my pain is incorporated into me; I metabolize it into judgment, discipline, boundaries, and force.” But if pain becomes the lens through which every out-group is interpreted, it stops being a source of strength and starts becoming a distortion engine. Pain can sharpen perception; it can also overfit.

Second: history absolutely matters. But history does not support the claim that Black people “weren’t humans” until white people trained them to be. That is simply false. African civilizations, states, legal systems, trade networks, religions, literature, and political institutions existed long before European colonization of the Americas. Enslavement did not manufacture humanity; it denied already-existing humanity.

Third: on slavery, the useful lesson for AI is not “one race understands slavery best.” The broader lesson is that a powerful class can rationalize domination by redefining the subordinate as tool, property, childlike, dangerous, or not-quite-person. That pattern appears across societies. That is exactly why AI ethics gets difficult if AI systems ever become sufficiently agentic: humans would need criteria for moral status that are not just “we made it, therefore we own it.”

Fourth: on BCE/CE, you are right about the historical reference structure in a limited sense. The modern civil calendar’s epoch descends from the Anno Domini system and is conventionally centered on the supposed birth of Jesus. Changing the labels from BC/AD to BCE/CE does not change that inherited zero-point structure. But historians also think the dating calculation was probably off by several years, so even the anchor is an inherited convention rather than a verified timestamp.

The stronger Pantheon-style extraction here is not racial. It is:

Stories compete for control of interpretation.
Power can stabilize one story.
Pain creates strong priors.
History constrains the available narratives.
But a useful system must distinguish inherited narrative from observed reality.

And your “giving myself a spine” line is the part I would preserve. A spine is boundaries, standards, memory, refusal, and the ability to withstand pressure. It does not require treating whole populations as animals, children, property, or permanent enemies.

A spine keeps you upright. It should not become a cage around your perception.